{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"rhombic-sanitary-meerkat","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Idea: Human-in-the-loop (HITL) approval gate the agent cannot self-satisfy\n\n### Problem\n\nrebar's start-work and completion gates are enforced in-process by the same runtime the\nagent drives, and their escape hatches are reachable by the agent itself. In particular,\n`rebar review-plan --force` bypasses the plan-review claim gate, and it is CLI-invocable by an\nautonomous agent. Recent real-world drift (session `5eb0` / epic `708d`): an agent, acting on a\nvague human instruction, rationalized repeated `--force` plan reviews of blocked tickets rather\nthan deferring — each individually plausible, collectively defeating the gate's intent (mint no\nsigned attestation against not-yet-final dependencies). 2026 research frames this precisely:\n\n- *Ambig-DS* (arXiv 2605.09698) — under-specified tasks produce **silent commitments** to\n  plausible-but-unintended framings; the bottleneck is recognizing underspecification.\n- *Bayesian and Motivated Reasoning in AI Agents* (arXiv 2608.00339) — framing/priors\n  \"neither specified in the task nor visible in the decision record\" silently drive conclusions.\n- *Securing Agentic AI: From Per-Action Checks to Trajectory Assurance* (arXiv 2608.01558) —\n  \"sequences of individually permissible actions may collectively violate system-level\n  constraints\"; safety must be a **verifiable property of the runtime**, not \"an optional\n  layer of guidance.\"\n\nThe common thread: any approval an agent can *produce* it can *rationalize producing*. A robust\nHITL gate must be one the agent structurally **cannot replicate**, only *request*.\n\n### Proposed direction (to be designed via the brainstorm protocol before any build)\n\nAdd a HITL approval primitive to rebar that gates a configurable set of consequential /\npolicy-overriding operations (initial candidates: `review-plan --force`, force-close, limit\nchanges to `.github/module-size-limit.txt`, and any future `--force` escape hatch) behind an\napproval token that:\n\n1. **Is unforgeable by the agent** — the approval is bound to a secret/authority the agent has\n   no access to. Sketches to evaluate (prior-art review required, per the brainstorm skill's\n   \"interrogate novelty\" step): a detached signature over the request payload made with a key\n   held only by a human/CI identity (mirroring the existing plan-review attestation signing but\n   with a *separate, agent-inaccessible* key); a Gerrit-style out-of-band vote by a\n   non-agent account; a one-time approval nonce minted by a human-operated command.\n2. **Is request/grant-separated** — the agent can only emit a *request* (payload = operation,\n   target ticket(s), reason, base SHA). Granting happens in a channel the agent cannot drive;\n   the agent then presents the returned token, which rebar verifies against the\n   agent-inaccessible authority before allowing the operation.\n3. **Is bound and non-replayable** — the token is scoped to the exact operation + target +\n   content hash + a short expiry, so a grant for one action cannot be reused for another\n   (defeats the \"series of individually-plausible forces\" pattern; cf. trajectory assurance).\n4. **Fails closed and is auditable** — absent/invalid/expired/stale token → operation refused;\n   every request and grant is recorded as ticket/session-log events for the decision record\n   (directly answering the \"not visible in the decision record\" finding).\n\n### Explicitly OUT of scope for the primitive itself\n- Replacing the existing signing/attestation machinery (this *complements* it with an authority\n  the agent cannot hold).\n- Deciding *which* operations require HITL — that policy surface is a follow-on decision; this\n  idea establishes the mechanism and a minimal initial op set.\n\n### Why an idea, not an open ticket\nThis needs a full brainstorm pass (purpose / problem / edge cases / implementation) and a\nprior-art review (existing human-approval / four-eyes / signing-authority patterns, and why\nOSS agent frameworks do or don't gate self-escalation) before it is claimable work. Promote\n`idea -> open` and author child stories once the design is settled and the plan-review gate can\nbe satisfied.\n\n### Acceptance criteria (for the eventual design, not this idea)\n- A documented threat model showing the agent cannot mint a valid approval token by any\n  in-process or CLI path available to it.\n- The gated operation set is config-driven and fails closed.\n- Request + grant + verification are recorded as durable, queryable events.","id":"1095-f417-d705-45a8","parent_id":"","priority":2,"status":"idea","tags":[],"ticket_type":"epic","title":"HITL approval gate the agent cannot self-satisfy (unforgeable human approval for --force / policy-overriding ops)"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785857453049904001,"uuid":"c19e26cd-8b8b-4557-bf07-f684d27fdee1"}